+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+This decision sits under the HLD's assignment of concurrency semantics to the record-store node: the HLD names record-store as the owner of how concurrent edits become a current record and leaves tablet-sync and review-export to be sized around the contract that node publishes.
+
+Field workers share 12 tablets and survey 320 permanent plots, producing about 6,000 plot-visit records per season. Tablets can be offline for 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. A visit carries observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some pairs of edits are unrelated — an observer-name typo and a newly added species — while others are scientifically related — a species identification and the cover estimate for that same species. The server cannot reliably infer these relationships from field names. A regional science partner receives a reviewed CSV every Friday, and two coordinators resolve questions about submitted observations.
+
+The reconciliation policy decides which independently submitted visit becomes the value in that CSV and how a reader can trace it back. Both policies under consideration retain every original submission (the HLD tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit") and keep the path from submission to export inspectable; the spreadsheet-style overwrite that discarded corrections is not a contender. Neither live option is eliminated by a binary constraint, so the choice turns on how the criteria below are weighted. At stake in each direction: a policy that combines related edits automatically can place a scientifically incoherent value in an export without anyone noticing; a policy that defers every concurrency event to a human can bury two coordinators in review work and leave visits out of a given Friday's CSV.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No binary gate eliminates either contender; both clear the inherited baseline above. The decision is made on weighted criteria. Importance: **major / moderate / minor**.
+
+- **C1 — Risk of a silently incoherent exported value (major).** Importance derives from the tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" and the HLD non-goal "Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent." A combination that joins a species-identification edit and a cover-estimate edit, treating them as disjoint fields, produces a value no intact submission ever contained.
+- **C2 — Review workload for two coordinators (major).** Importance derives from the tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator." Roughly 6,000 visits per season must clear review before a weekly CSV.
+- **C3 — Maintenance burden of reconciliation logic (moderate).** Importance derives from the same operability tenet: maintained rules are ongoing code that must stay correct as fields evolve.
+- **C4 — Export completeness per Friday cycle (moderate).** How many visits reach the reviewed CSV without waiting on a human resolution.
+- **C5 — Submission size and sync cost (minor).** Complete visit snapshots versus structured field deltas over 48-hour offline windows on shared tablets.
+- **C6 — Field-worker re-work (moderate).** Whether a worker's unrelated correction survives into the current value or must be re-entered after another edit wins.
+
+C2, C4, C5, and C6 cut against the option ultimately selected; all four favor field-aware merging.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission is a complete visit snapshot naming the base revision it was derived from. Two or more submissions derived from the same base become competing revisions; the visit is unresolved and held out of the export until a coordinator selects one competing revision as current. The store performs no field-level combination, so every current value is an intact worker submission. Every submission, including losing revisions, is retained with author and receipt time; a resolution records the coordinator, the selected revision, and the contributing revision IDs.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers fetch revision R7 of a visit. Worker A corrects an observer name; worker B adds a missing species. Both submit complete visits naming R7. They are stored as competing revisions R8a and R8b; review compares the two whole visits; the coordinator selects one. The other remains in history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- C1: **strong** — the current value is always one intact submission; no combination can join related fields.
+- C2: **weak** — every pair of concurrent edits, including trivially unrelated ones, creates a review item.
+- C3: **strong** — no merge rules to author or maintain.
+- C4: **weak** — unresolved visits are excluded from the CSV until a coordinator acts.
+- C5: **weak** — each edit transmits a full visit snapshot rather than a delta.
+- C6: **weak** — a losing revision's unrelated correction is not in the current value and must be re-entered.
+
+**Summary.** Guarantees an intact, inspectable exported value at the cost of review volume, export latency, sync size, and some re-work.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base snapshot and a structured set of field-level changes. The server three-way merges them: edits to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new current value, and only overlapping changes to the same field are flagged for review. Maintained rules define combination for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and field-level provenance records which submission contributed each value. Every original submission is retained.
+
+**Why this approach remains credible.** It is the option a team would still seriously consider, and would prefer if review volume rather than combination risk were the binding constraint. Disjoint edits to unrelated fields need no human judgment, so a coordinator is only asked about genuine conflicts; more visits are therefore ready for each Friday's CSV without manual resolution. Field workers see both unrelated corrections applied rather than watching one win a whole-visit contest, and neither correction has to be re-entered. Structured deltas are smaller than complete snapshots, which matters over 48-hour offline windows and on shared local storage. Field-level provenance also gives a finer-grained audit of how each exported field arose than a single selected revision does.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name; worker B adds a missing species. The changes touch disjoint fields and are combined automatically with no review item. Only a same-field conflict — two edits to the same species' cover estimate — is sent to a coordinator.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- C1: **weak** — the rule set operates on field identity, not scientific relationships; it can combine a species identification and its cover estimate as disjoint fields.
+- C2: **strong** — only overlapping changes create review work; trivial unrelated edits resolve themselves.
+- C3: **weak** — scalar, list, and species-entry rules must be authored and kept correct as fields evolve.
+- C4: **strong** — visits with only disjoint edits are export-ready without a coordinator.
+- C5: **strong** — structured deltas are smaller than complete visit snapshots.
+- C6: **strong** — disjoint unrelated corrections both survive into the current value.
+
+**Summary.** Minimizes review work, export latency, sync size, and re-work; relies on rules that cannot detect scientific relationships between fields.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions is selected. It wins on **C1**, the major criterion: a single undetected scientifically incoherent value in the partner's CSV is a worse failure than additional review toil, because the team has no automated way to notice a combination that crossed a relationship the merge rules did not model. The decision turns on the tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" — an intact, selected revision can be read back to one worker's submission, whereas a rule-combined value cannot — and on "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator," which the absence of maintained merge rules serves.
+
+Stated plainly, this selection goes against the majority of the criteria: C2, C4, C5, and C6 all favor field-aware merging, and the decision accepts a larger coordinator review queue, lower per-Friday export completeness, larger sync payloads, and some field-worker re-work as the price. The outcome rests on the weighting of C1 above those costs, not on the analysis favoring the chosen option overall. It is a deliberate judgment, not a conclusion the criteria force.
+
+**What the decision forecloses.** Automatic combination of disjoint field edits, any field-level provenance of a combined value, and the possibility that trivially unrelated concurrent edits resolve without a human.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.** The tablet and export work must accommodate the following, which this decision fixes but those nodes own:
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain the base revision ID for every local draft across restarts and multi-day disconnection; submit complete visit snapshots rather than field deltas, sizing local storage and the upload/retry state machine (idempotency key plus base revision ID) around full-visit payloads; and surface pending competing revisions to field workers so a worker understands why a submitted edit is not yet the current value. It must also define behavior when a worker edits again while a prior submission is still unresolved.
+- **Review-export** must provide a revision-comparison workflow that shows competing complete visit snapshots side by side so a coordinator can select one; hold every unresolved visit out of the reviewed CSV; and carry the selected revision and contributing revision IDs through to the resolution record. Both must remain operable by two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator.
+
+**When to re-run the judgment.** Re-weigh if review volume grows beyond what two coordinators can absorb (moves C2/C4), or if field-combination rules can be made aware of scientific relationships between fields (moves C1). Either change could reverse the weighting.
