+## Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+Each submission is a complete visit snapshot, not a field delta. A worker's submitted visit is retained intact as a unit.
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+- **Revision** — a stored, complete visit snapshot with a server-assigned revision ID. The first submission for a new visit is the root revision and names no base.
+- **Base revision ID** — the revision a worker edited from. Every non-root submission names the base revision it was derived from.
+- **Competing revisions** — two or more submissions naming the same base revision. They cannot both be the current state, so the visit becomes unresolved and carries review work.
+- **Current revision** — the single revision retrieval reports as the visit's current state.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+A submission naming the current revision as its base, with no competing sibling, becomes the new current revision without review. Competing revisions are held for review; the store does not choose among them by arrival time, author, or field. The store performs no field-level combination.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Submission Envelope
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+An accepted submission records: visit ID, the complete visit payload (plot reference and scientific fields), the base revision ID (or none for a root revision), author ID, server receipt time, idempotency key, and a server-assigned revision ID. Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged. Each stored revision is the worker's original payload; a resolution records a selection among revisions and never rewrites one.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Current State and Review Work
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Current-state retrieval returns the current revision and an indicator of whether unresolved review work exists. While competing revisions are unresolved, retrieval reports the last agreed current revision together with the set of competing revision IDs. Submission-history retrieval returns every stored revision in receipt order with its author and receipt time. The unresolved-work representation is what review-export consumes to decide export eligibility.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Resolution
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by selecting one competing revision as the current revision. The resolution retains the coordinator's identity, the selected revision ID, and the contributing revision IDs with the outcome. Losing revisions remain in history; none is discarded. A visit with no unresolved work is eligible for downstream export.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| [Reconciling concurrent edits](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md) | Whole-visit revisions: each submission is a complete revision naming its base; competing revisions go to coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge (auto-combine disjoint field edits, review only overlaps) | Field-aware merging needs maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and can combine edits that are scientifically related — a species identification and its cover estimate — without recognizing the relationship. Whole-visit revisions keep each worker's submission intact and require no merge rules. The cost is a larger review queue: competing edits to unrelated fields still produce review work. Two coordinators can absorb that queue. |
+
+## Downstream Contract Obligations
+
+These obligations fall on sibling nodes; this node publishes the envelope and unresolved-work representation they consume and does not define their local behavior.
+
+- tablet-sync: retain base revision IDs for local drafts, submit complete visit snapshots, and surface pending competing revisions to field workers.
+- review-export: provide a revision-comparison workflow for coordinators and exclude unresolved visits from the reviewed CSV.
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
